The '''2024–25 Premier League''' was the 33rd season of the [[Premier League]] and the 126th season of {{nowrap|top-flight}} [[Football in England|English football]] overall. [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] entered the season as [[Premier League records and statistics#Titles|four-time defending champions]], but were dethroned by [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], who emerged as Premier League winners with four games to spare, equalling [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]'s record of [[List of English football champions|20 English league titles]].

The fixtures were released on 18 June 2024, consisting of 33 weekend rounds, four midweek rounds, and one Bank Holiday matchweek.<ref>{{cite web |title=Fixture release date for 2024/25 season announced |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3977771 |website=premierleague.com |date=23 April 2024 |publisher=Premier League |access-date=25 April 2024 |archive-date=25 April 2024 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20240425162730/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3977771 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The summer transfer window opened on 14 June 2024 and closed at 23:00 [[British Summer Time|BST]] on 30 August 2024. The winter window opened on 1 January 2025 and closed at 23:00 [[Greenwich Mean Time|GMT]] on 3 February 2025.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/4012986 |title=Dates for summer 2024 and winter 2025 transfer windows confirmed |publisher=Premier League |website=premierleague.com |date=15 May 2024 |access-date=16 May 2024 |archive-date=14 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240614170449/https://www.premierleague.com/news/4012986 |url-status=live }}</ref> This was also the last season the [[Nike Inc.|Nike]] sponsor match ball for the top flight was used; [[Puma (brand)|Puma]] became the official ball supplier of the top flight starting from the 2025–26 season.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Haidarovic |first=Luke |title=PUMA Becomes Official Partner Of The Premier League {{!}} PUMA® |url=https://about.puma.com/en/newsroom/corporate-news/2025/17-03-2025-puma-becomes-official-partner-premier-league |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250318082444/https://about.puma.com/en/newsroom/corporate-news/2025/17-03-2025-puma-becomes-official-partner-premier-league |archive-date=18 March 2025 |access-date=17 March 2025 |website=[[Puma (brand)|Puma]] |language=en}}</ref>

==Summary==
The first managerial departure of the season came on 28 October 2024, when [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] sacked [[Erik ten Hag]] following a 2–1 defeat to [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]]. The club sat in 14th place after nine games, having already recorded their fourth defeat of the season.<ref name="Ten Hag">{{cite news|publisher=Manchester United F.C.|url=https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/man-utd-official-club-statement-on-erik-ten-hag-28-oct|title=Man Utd official club statement on Erik ten Hag|date=28 October 2024|access-date=28 October 2024|archive-date=9 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241109203418/https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/man-utd-official-club-statement-on-erik-ten-hag-28-oct|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Ruben Amorim]] was announced as his successor on 1 November and officially took charge on 11 November.<ref name="Amorim">{{Cite web |title=Man Utd appoint Ruben Amorim as new head coach |url=https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/man-utd-appoint-ruben-amorim-as-new-head-coach |date=1 November 2024 |access-date=1 November 2024 |publisher=Manchester United F.C.}}</ref>  

The second managerial departure occurred on 24 November, when [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] sacked [[Steve Cooper (football manager)|Steve Cooper]] following a 2–1 home loss to [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]. The club sat in 16th place at the time and one point above the relegation zone, having already suffered their sixth loss of the season and having managed only two wins from 12 games.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Leicester City Parts Company With Steve Cooper |url=https://www.lcfc.com/news/4175174?lang=en |access-date=24 November 2024 |website=www.lcfc.com |language=en |archive-date=24 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241124171600/https://www.lcfc.com/news/4175174?lang=EN |url-status=live }}</ref> On 29 November, [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] announced the appointment of [[Ruud van Nistelrooy]] on a three-year contract.<ref name="van Nistelrooy" />

[[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]] dismissed head coach [[Gary O'Neil]] on 15 December after a 2–1 home defeat to [[Ipswich Town F.C.|Ipswich Town]]. It was their 11th loss in 16 games; the club was 19th in the table, four points from safety, with only two wins.<ref name="Gary O'Neil">{{Cite web |title=Wolves sack manager O'Neil after Ipswich defeat |date=15 December 2024 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cpvzvd7z3vno |access-date=15 December 2024 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |archive-date=15 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241215123817/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cpvzvd7z3vno |url-status=live }}</ref> He was replaced by [[Vítor Pereira (footballer, born 1968)|Vitor Pereira]]. On the same day, bottom side [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] sacked [[Russell Martin (footballer)|Russell Martin]] immediately after a 5–0 home defeat to Tottenham Hotspur.<ref name="Russell Martin">{{Cite web |title=Russell Martin: Southampton sack manager Martin after Spurs thrashing |date= 15 December 2024 |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c98eg5z8md3o |access-date=15 December 2024 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] }}</ref> He was replaced by [[Ivan Jurić]].

On 8 January 2025, West Ham United sacked [[Julen Lopetegui]] with the club sitting in 14th place. His final match in charge was a 4–1 away defeat to [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]]. [[Graham Potter]] was confirmed as his replacement on 9 January.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 January 2025 |title=Graham Potter appointed new West Ham manager after Julen Lopetegui sacking |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cy535e92zezo |access-date=9 January 2025 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref> On the same day, [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] sacked [[Sean Dyche]] just hours before their [[2024–25 FA Cup|FA Cup]] match. His last match was a 1–0 away defeat to [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]], with the club sitting in 16th place.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Club statement |url=https://www.evertonfc.com/news/2025/january/09/club-statement/ |access-date=9 January 2025 |website=Everton F.C.}}</ref> On 11 January, [[David Moyes]] was confirmed as his replacement, returning to the club 12 years after leaving in 2013 to replace the retiring [[Alex Ferguson|Sir Alex Ferguson]] at Manchester United.<ref name="Moyes">{{Cite web |title=Moyes Returns to Everton |url=https://www.evertonfc.com/news/2025/january/11/moyes-returns-to-everton/ |access-date=11 January 2025 |website=Everton F.C. |archive-date=11 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250111092201/https://www.evertonfc.com/news/2025/january/11/moyes-returns-to-everton |url-status=live }}</ref>

Southampton became the first team relegated to the [[EFL Championship]] on 6 April, after just one year in the top flight, following a 3–1 defeat away to Tottenham Hotspur. With only 10 points from 31 games, they became the first team in Premier League history to go down with seven matches left to play, breaking the record shared by [[Derby County F.C.|Derby County]] in 2007–08, [[Huddersfield Town A.F.C.|Huddersfield Town]] in 2018–19 and [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]] in 2020–21, who were all relegated with six matches remaining.<ref name="Southampton relegated">{{Cite web |last=Brotherton |first=Alex |title=Tottenham Hotspur 3–1 Southampton: Saints relegated in record time |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/live/crkxdgxzdvmt |access-date=6 April 2025 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] }}</ref> The next day, the club announced that [[Ivan Jurić]] had left his role as manager after just 108 days in charge, having won only two games during his spell (one in the Premier League and one in the [[FA Cup]]). [[Simon Rusk]] took over as interim until the end of the season with Southampton midfielder [[Adam Lallana]] becoming Rusk's assistant.<ref name="Ivan Juric">{{Cite web |title=A statement from the board of Southampton FC |url=https://www.southamptonfc.com/en/news/article/a-statement-from-the-board-of-southampton-fc-apr-2025 |access-date=7 April 2025 |website=[[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] |date=7 April 2025 |archive-date=7 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407112437/https://www.southamptonfc.com/en/news/article/a-statement-from-the-board-of-southampton-fc-apr-2025 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] became the second team to be relegated on 20 April after a 1–0 defeat at home to [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], which extended their record for most consecutive goalless home matches, making them the first club in top-flight history to fail to score in nine consecutive home matches.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McNulty|first=Phill |title='Leicester City 0–1 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/live/c70584ql70rt#Report |access-date=20 April 2025 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]]}}</ref> Six days later, [[Ipswich Town F.C.|Ipswich Town]] joined them to become the third and final team relegated, after a 3–0 loss to [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]], meaning that for the second season in a row, all three promoted teams went straight back down. It was only the third time in English top flight history in which this has happened, the first being in [[1997–98 FA Premier League|1997–98]]. This also marked the first time in the Premier League era that all three relegated teams had been confirmed with more than four games to play, breaking the record of more than two games remaining, set in the [[2020–21 Premier League|2020–21 season]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Howarth |first=Matthew |title='Newcastle United 3–0 Ipswich Town |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c0qn44yg32wt |access-date=26 April 2025 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |archive-date=23 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423234929/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c0qn44yg32wt |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="opta-relegation">{{Cite web |last=Tweedale |first=Ali |date=26 April 2025 |title=The Three Promoted Premier League Sides Will Be Relegated Again – This Never Used to Happen |url=https://theanalyst.com/2025/04/premier-league-promoted-sides-relegation-championship |access-date=27 April 2025 |website=Opta Analyst |language=en |archive-date=10 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250410140456/https://theanalyst.com/2025/04/premier-league-promoted-sides-relegation-championship |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 27 April, [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] beat [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] 5–1 at Anfield to secure their second Premier League title with four games remaining, tying the [[List of English football champions|record]] for total English league titles of 20 with [[Liverpool F.C.–Manchester United F.C. rivalry|rivals]] Manchester United.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Liverpool vs Tottenham Hotspur: Premier League preview, team news, stats & head-to-head |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cj3x2ee1261t#Report |access-date=27 April 2025 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB |archive-date=24 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250424041223/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cj3x2ee1261t#Report |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 18 May, and after 133 years, [[Goodison Park]] hosted its final Everton game, with the club moving to their new home (the [[Hill Dickinson Stadium]]), from the [[2025–26 Everton F.C. season|2025–26 season]]. Everton eased to a 2–0 win over Southampton with [[Iliman Ndiaye]] scoring both goals, sealing his place in history as the last goalscorer at the stadium.<ref>{{cite web| title=Goodison Park: Emotional scenes mark Everton's last game at old ground| date=18 May 2025| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cly31g20ygvo}}</ref> From the [[2025–26 Everton F.C. (women) season|2025–26 season]], Goodison Park became the permanent home for [[Everton F.C. (women)|Everton Women's team]] and the largest dedicated women's football stadium in England.<ref>{{cite web |title=Goodison to Become Permanent Home of Everton Women |url=https://www.evertonfc.com/news/2025/may/13/goodison-park-to-become-permanent-home-of-everton-women/ |website=www.evertonfc.com |access-date=22 October 2025 |date=13 May 2025}}</ref>

On 25 May, Liverpool were presented with the Premier League trophy after a 1–1 draw with [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] on the final day of the season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2025/may/25/liverpool-crystal-palace-premier-league-match-report|title= Liverpool share points with Crystal Palace before getting party started|date=25 May 2025|work=Guardian|accessdate=28 May 2025}}</ref>

=== Developments ===
The two-week winter break, in effect since the 2019–20 season, was scrapped starting this season in favour of longer intervals between matches being added for the holiday period between Christmas and New Year's Day, with no matches held on Christmas Eve.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 December 2023 |title=PL opts out of Christmas Eve games for '24–25 |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/39167977/premier-league-opts-christmas-eve-games-2024-2025 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240228023759/https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/39167977/premier-league-opts-christmas-eve-games-2024-2025 |archive-date=28 February 2024 |access-date=28 February 2024 |website=ESPN.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Meade |first=Samuel |date=23 December 2023 |title=Premier League make Christmas Eve decision for next season amid Chelsea backlash |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/sport/football/news/premier-league-christmas-eve-fixture-31738611 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240515204156/https://www.mirror.co.uk/sport/football/news/premier-league-christmas-eve-fixture-31738611 |archive-date=15 May 2024 |access-date=28 February 2024 |website=Daily Mirror}}</ref>

This season was the first to use [[semi-automated offside technology]], as Premier League clubs unanimously agreed to its introduction. The technology was planned to be introduced after one of the autumn international breaks,<ref>{{cite news |date=11 April 2024 |title=Premier League statement on Semi-Automated Offside Technology |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3962262 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240606215329/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3962262 |archive-date=6 June 2024 |access-date=18 April 2024 |publisher=Premier League}}</ref> but was delayed to further test the technology. It was tested in the FA Cup fifth round onwards,<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 February 2025 |title=FA Cup to use semi-automated offsides for first time |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cx2k9klnpvlo |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250215053354/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cx2k9klnpvlo |archive-date=15 February 2025 |access-date=15 February 2025 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref> after which the Premier League confirmed that the technology would be used in Matchweek 32, on 12 April 2025. The [[City of Manchester Stadium|Etihad Stadium]] was the first to officially use the technology, during Manchester City's match against [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 2025 |title=Semi-automated offside technology to be introduced in Matchweek 32 |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/4273447 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250406153359/https://www.premierleague.com/news/4273447 |archive-date=6 April 2025 |access-date=5 April 2025 |website=www.premierleague.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 April 2025 |title=Semi-automated offside explained: Technology starts in Premier League |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/czrvvvpldeyo |access-date=13 April 2025 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref>

==Teams==
Twenty teams competed in the league – the top seventeen teams from the previous season and the three teams promoted from the [[2023–24 EFL Championship|Championship]]. The promoted teams were [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]], [[Ipswich Town F.C.|Ipswich Town]] and [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]]. Leicester City and Southampton returned after a one-year absence, while Ipswich Town returned after a twenty-two-year absence. They replaced [[Luton Town F.C.|Luton Town]], [[Burnley F.C.|Burnley]] and [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]], who were all relegated to the [[2024–25 EFL Championship|Championship]] after just one season in the top flight, the first time since the [[1997–98 FA Premier League|1997–98]] season that all three promoted teams were relegated after just one season.<ref name="opta-relegation"/>

